1
00:01:44,705 --> 00:01:46,537
Ms. Spalding?

2
00:01:47,741 --> 00:01:51,200
Ms. Spalding, it's just
me. Forgot my watch.

3
00:02:06,660 --> 00:02:08,026
Ms. Spalding?

4
00:02:08,195 --> 00:02:11,563
Ms. Spalding, you want me
to bring your dinner upstairs?

5
00:02:27,814 --> 00:02:29,339
Ms. Spalding?

6
00:02:35,188 --> 00:02:36,918
Ms. Spalding?

7
00:03:08,355 --> 00:03:12,383
- Oh! Oh, mail, huh? Ha-ha-ha.
- Mm-hm.

8
00:03:12,559 --> 00:03:15,723
Occupant. Occupant.

9
00:03:15,896 --> 00:03:18,957
Occupant. Occupant.

10
00:03:21,435 --> 00:03:22,596
What's funny?

11
00:03:22,769 --> 00:03:24,465
Conrad sent me a birthday card.

12
00:03:24,638 --> 00:03:26,630
Yeah? Oh, he sent it here, huh?

13
00:03:32,045 --> 00:03:33,843
Happy birthday.

14
00:03:34,014 --> 00:03:38,952
Oh, Dad, you didn't have
to buy me a birthday gift.

15
00:03:39,119 --> 00:03:41,645
But if you hadn't, I
would've killed you.

16
00:03:52,032 --> 00:03:53,056
Oh.

17
00:03:54,468 --> 00:03:57,700
A cap. How fun.

18
00:03:59,339 --> 00:04:02,867
I thought you could use it.

19
00:04:05,278 --> 00:04:07,645
Next time we go
fishing, of course.

20
00:04:07,814 --> 00:04:09,442
- Yeah, that'll be great.
- Yeah.

21
00:04:09,616 --> 00:04:14,111
If we do, you might
need a new fly rod.

22
00:04:14,888 --> 00:04:16,584
Oh, Dad.

23
00:04:16,757 --> 00:04:19,886
Oh, it's beautiful. I love it.

24
00:04:20,060 --> 00:04:22,359
Well, we have to go fishing
now. How about next weekend?

25
00:04:22,529 --> 00:04:25,590
It's all set. Meyers
Lake, you and me.

26
00:04:25,866 --> 00:04:27,266
Oh!

27
00:04:27,534 --> 00:04:31,027
You are the greatest. Whoo!

28
00:04:33,540 --> 00:04:37,500
- Oh, no.
- What?

29
00:04:39,379 --> 00:04:41,007
I've been called for jury duty.

30
00:04:43,483 --> 00:04:45,714
You don't suppose...?

31
00:04:45,886 --> 00:04:48,913
- Our trip.
- Oh, come on, Dad.

32
00:04:49,089 --> 00:04:52,924
What prosecutor in his right
mind would want you on his jury?

33
00:04:53,093 --> 00:04:55,119
You're right. You're right.

34
00:04:55,295 --> 00:04:59,858
There's not a snowball's
chance in hell I'll ever be on a jury.

35
00:05:00,467 --> 00:05:04,165
McGONlGAL: As you are aware,
Mrs. O'Malley, this is a murder case.

36
00:05:04,337 --> 00:05:08,331
Which means that if the state
proves beyond a reasonable doubt

37
00:05:08,508 --> 00:05:11,706
that Travis Spalding, with
premeditation, murdered his wife,

38
00:05:11,878 --> 00:05:13,437
it'd be your duty
to find him guilty.

39
00:05:13,947 --> 00:05:16,348
Now, conceivably, he could
receive the death penalty.

40
00:05:16,516 --> 00:05:20,954
Now, knowing that, do you believe
you could render a guilty verdict?

41
00:05:22,322 --> 00:05:24,154
Yes, I believe I could.

42
00:05:26,693 --> 00:05:30,687
So do I. Thank
you Mrs. O'Malley.

43
00:05:32,432 --> 00:05:34,992
Juror six, Ben Matlock.

44
00:05:35,168 --> 00:05:36,888
- Mr. Whiteman.
- Your Honor.

45
00:05:37,604 --> 00:05:40,438
Defense is very well-acquainted
with Mr. Matlock's qualifications.

46
00:05:40,607 --> 00:05:44,567
I have no reservations that he
could render a fair and just verdict.

47
00:05:45,145 --> 00:05:48,274
I gladly accept him
as a juror on this case.

48
00:05:48,748 --> 00:05:49,841
Mr. McGonigal.

49
00:05:53,320 --> 00:05:55,118
Afternoon, Mr. Matlock.

50
00:05:56,289 --> 00:05:58,724
How's it feel to be
sitting over there?

51
00:05:59,593 --> 00:06:02,654
- Strange. McGONlGAL:
I can imagine.

52
00:06:02,829 --> 00:06:04,661
Bet you think I'm
gonna dismiss you.

53
00:06:06,299 --> 00:06:08,564
Well, I'd understand if you did.

54
00:06:10,704 --> 00:06:15,108
Can you imagine the shrieks of
prosecutors all over this country?

55
00:06:15,275 --> 00:06:17,403
"McGonigal, you
idiot," they'd say.

56
00:06:17,577 --> 00:06:19,546
"Why, Ben Matlock
is one of the greatest

57
00:06:19,713 --> 00:06:22,273
criminal defense
lawyers in the nation."

58
00:06:22,449 --> 00:06:28,081
The truth is, any other case, I'd
probably bounce you like a bad check.

59
00:06:28,255 --> 00:06:31,248
This case against
Travis Spalding's different.

60
00:06:32,792 --> 00:06:35,591
It's air tight. No holes.

61
00:06:35,762 --> 00:06:36,923
Got it all:

62
00:06:37,097 --> 00:06:42,263
Motive, direct irrefutable
evidence, opportunity.

63
00:06:42,435 --> 00:06:48,033
Now all I need to win this
case are 12 sensible people

64
00:06:48,208 --> 00:06:51,235
who understand the
notion of reasonable doubt.

65
00:06:52,345 --> 00:06:54,280
Now, I figure who
better than Ben Matlock

66
00:06:54,447 --> 00:06:56,848
understands the meaning
of reasonable doubt?

67
00:06:57,017 --> 00:07:01,421
Well, I have a pretty good
idea of what it is and what it isn't.

68
00:07:01,588 --> 00:07:04,990
So should the state prove
beyond a reasonable doubt

69
00:07:05,158 --> 00:07:06,922
that Travis Spalding
murdered his wife,

70
00:07:07,093 --> 00:07:10,825
could you act reasonably
and find him guilty?

71
00:07:12,198 --> 00:07:14,633
Well, I suppose, yeah.

72
00:07:14,801 --> 00:07:17,771
Yeah, I mean, if you
got the evidence and...

73
00:07:32,152 --> 00:07:35,680
Ms. Carpenter, you were the Spalding
housekeeper for how many years?

74
00:07:35,855 --> 00:07:38,381
Three and a half.

75
00:07:38,558 --> 00:07:40,390
McGONlGAL: Quite
a bit of time with them.

76
00:07:40,560 --> 00:07:44,793
How would you characterize
their relationship, their marriage?

77
00:07:46,132 --> 00:07:49,432
Well, the first year
or two, it was okay.

78
00:07:49,602 --> 00:07:52,731
Not like newlyweds or
anything. They fought a lot.

79
00:07:53,239 --> 00:07:54,434
McGONlGAL: About what?

80
00:07:54,607 --> 00:07:56,371
Not long after
they were married,

81
00:07:56,543 --> 00:07:59,274
Ms. Spalding developed
a real bad back problem.

82
00:07:59,446 --> 00:08:03,247
She must have had five surgeries.
Couldn't even walk without her cane.

83
00:08:03,416 --> 00:08:06,284
Anyway, she spent a
lot of time at home in bed.

84
00:08:06,453 --> 00:08:09,548
She just wanted Mr. Spalding to
spend more time with her, that's all.

85
00:08:09,723 --> 00:08:13,717
Where did Mr. Spalding
spend his time, at work?

86
00:08:13,893 --> 00:08:17,227
Oh, no, Mr. Spalding don't work.
He lives off Ms. Spalding's money.

87
00:08:17,397 --> 00:08:19,298
He does play a lot of golf.

88
00:08:20,100 --> 00:08:22,797
Recently had they
fought more frequently?

89
00:08:22,969 --> 00:08:24,164
Yes, sir.

90
00:08:25,238 --> 00:08:28,800
Ms. Spalding suspected that
Mr. Spalding was having an affair.

91
00:08:29,809 --> 00:08:33,473
So she hired a private investigator
to find out whether he was or not.

92
00:08:35,315 --> 00:08:36,647
Was he?

93
00:08:36,816 --> 00:08:38,580
Oh, yes, sir.

94
00:08:39,519 --> 00:08:42,114
- Then what happened?
- Well, they had a huge fight.

95
00:08:42,288 --> 00:08:43,347
McGONlGAL: When was this?

96
00:08:43,523 --> 00:08:45,890
Ten days before
Ms. Spalding died.

97
00:08:46,059 --> 00:08:49,962
McGONlGAL: Did you hear this fight?
- Oh, yes, sir. It was very loud.

98
00:08:50,130 --> 00:08:51,223
What was said?

99
00:08:51,398 --> 00:08:54,425
Well, Ms. Spalding told him
that she was filing for divorce.

100
00:08:54,601 --> 00:08:56,399
Then she kicked him
right out of the house.

101
00:08:57,170 --> 00:09:00,197
McGONlGAL: And how did
Mr. Spalding react to this news?

102
00:09:00,373 --> 00:09:03,138
Well, he was as angry
as I've ever seen him.

103
00:09:03,309 --> 00:09:06,541
Said he didn't care about any prenuptial
agreements that they had signed

104
00:09:06,713 --> 00:09:09,444
and that he was not leaving that
marriage without his fair share.

105
00:09:09,616 --> 00:09:12,711
McGONlGAL: Did
Mr. Spalding threaten his wife?

106
00:09:12,886 --> 00:09:17,654
Yes, sir. He said he would kill her
before he would let her divorce him.

107
00:09:19,259 --> 00:09:20,625
Nothing further.

108
00:09:20,794 --> 00:09:23,286
Your witness, Mr. Whiteman.

109
00:09:24,464 --> 00:09:29,334
Ms. Carpenter, you dislike
Mr. Spalding, now, don't you?

110
00:09:31,071 --> 00:09:32,539
Uh...

111
00:09:33,206 --> 00:09:36,665
I think Mr. Spalding married
Mrs. Spalding for her money.

112
00:09:36,843 --> 00:09:38,141
He didn't really love her.

113
00:09:38,878 --> 00:09:42,542
And you'd love to see him go to
prison for Mrs. Spalding's murder.

114
00:09:42,715 --> 00:09:43,944
Now, wouldn't you?

115
00:09:44,117 --> 00:09:46,086
Well, I think he did it.
- Really?

116
00:09:46,619 --> 00:09:49,088
Well, why don't you
tell us what happened

117
00:09:49,255 --> 00:09:51,656
the night you found
Mrs. Spalding's body,

118
00:09:51,825 --> 00:09:56,889
so that we can all understand why
you're so convinced that he did it?

119
00:09:57,063 --> 00:09:59,430
Now, it was your day off.

120
00:09:59,599 --> 00:10:04,264
And you had come back home
because you had forgotten your watch?

121
00:10:04,437 --> 00:10:07,430
That's right. And I was
on my way to my room

122
00:10:07,607 --> 00:10:09,337
when I went through
the dining room.

123
00:10:09,509 --> 00:10:13,037
And that was strange, because
Ms. Spalding had made herself dinner,

124
00:10:13,213 --> 00:10:15,705
but it was still sitting on the
table. She hadn't touched it.

125
00:10:15,882 --> 00:10:18,215
And that's when I called
upstairs on the intercom

126
00:10:18,384 --> 00:10:20,184
to see if she wanted
me to bring it up to her.

127
00:10:20,353 --> 00:10:23,585
And when she didn't answer,
is that when you went upstairs?

128
00:10:23,756 --> 00:10:24,780
Yes, sir.

129
00:10:24,958 --> 00:10:27,860
And that's when I heard the footsteps
running down the back stairway.

130
00:10:28,027 --> 00:10:30,519
And you thought
that was Mr. Spalding.

131
00:10:30,697 --> 00:10:33,064
Yes, sir. WHITEMAN: I see.

132
00:10:33,233 --> 00:10:35,065
Did you see him?

133
00:10:36,035 --> 00:10:37,595
- No.
- Did you hear his voice?

134
00:10:37,770 --> 00:10:40,137
See his car drive away?
Smell his aftershave?

135
00:10:41,040 --> 00:10:42,167
No, sir.

136
00:10:42,342 --> 00:10:47,246
So, what you're trying to tell us
is that on the night of the murder,

137
00:10:47,413 --> 00:10:51,111
you didn't hear a voice
or see one single thing.

138
00:10:51,818 --> 00:10:56,882
Please, tell the court why
you're so convinced that he did it.

139
00:10:57,690 --> 00:10:59,716
Because he threatened her life.

140
00:11:00,727 --> 00:11:07,463
Ms. Carpenter, if everyone
who threatened a life went to jail,

141
00:11:07,634 --> 00:11:11,127
half this courtroom would
probably be behind bars.

142
00:11:13,673 --> 00:11:15,005
Nothing further.

143
00:11:17,610 --> 00:11:20,512
Dr. Roberts, you're a medical
examiner for the city of Atlanta?

144
00:11:20,680 --> 00:11:21,909
Yes, I am.

145
00:11:22,081 --> 00:11:24,676
And you performed an
autopsy on Lenore Spalding?

146
00:11:24,851 --> 00:11:27,787
- Yes.
- What was the cause of death?

147
00:11:27,954 --> 00:11:30,651
Mrs. Spalding died from
a severe blow to the head,

148
00:11:30,823 --> 00:11:35,124
sustained when it hit the tile
bathtub steps in her bathroom.

149
00:11:36,029 --> 00:11:39,397
In your opinion, could Lenore
Spalding's death have been accidental?

150
00:11:39,566 --> 00:11:41,194
I don't believe so, no.

151
00:11:41,367 --> 00:11:43,768
Well, why not? She had a
bad back, had trouble walking.

152
00:11:43,937 --> 00:11:46,372
I found traces of ether
on Mrs. Spalding's face.

153
00:11:46,539 --> 00:11:50,271
Mrs. Spalding was
drugged before she died.

154
00:11:54,247 --> 00:11:57,115
After the housekeeper, Mrs.
Carpenter, told us she heard footsteps

155
00:11:57,283 --> 00:11:59,343
running down the back
stairs and into the garage,

156
00:11:59,519 --> 00:12:02,489
I had my men make a
thorough check of the area.

157
00:12:02,655 --> 00:12:04,180
What did your men
find, lieutenant?

158
00:12:04,357 --> 00:12:07,850
In the garage, they found Travis
Spalding's monogrammed handkerchief

159
00:12:08,027 --> 00:12:10,690
stuffed in the
pouch of his golf bag.

160
00:12:10,863 --> 00:12:12,889
It was soaked with ether.

161
00:12:14,500 --> 00:12:19,996
Wasn't ether found by the medical
examiner on Mrs. Spalding's face?

162
00:12:20,173 --> 00:12:23,575
Yes. It looked like she had
probably been drugged while in bed,

163
00:12:23,743 --> 00:12:24,802
taken to the bathroom,

164
00:12:24,978 --> 00:12:28,073
where her head was smashed
against the tile bathtub steps

165
00:12:28,748 --> 00:12:32,048
in the hope that it would appear as
though she had an accident and fell.

166
00:12:32,218 --> 00:12:35,017
McGONlGAL: Did you have a
chance to talk to Mr. Spalding that night?

167
00:12:35,555 --> 00:12:36,648
Yes, I did.

168
00:12:37,757 --> 00:12:40,022
Lieutenant, did you
ask Mr. Spalding

169
00:12:40,193 --> 00:12:42,685
when was the last time
he'd been at his wife's house?

170
00:12:42,862 --> 00:12:45,957
Yes, he said that he hadn't been
inside the house for five days.

171
00:12:46,132 --> 00:12:50,536
McGONlGAL: Did you later determine
that Mr. Spalding's story was untrue?

172
00:12:50,703 --> 00:12:52,729
Yes, the following day,
I received a lab report

173
00:12:52,905 --> 00:12:56,706
which indicated that Mr. Spalding's
fingerprints were found

174
00:12:56,876 --> 00:13:01,314
both on the outside and the inside
knob of the door leading to the garage.

175
00:13:02,348 --> 00:13:04,579
Couldn't those fingerprints
have been left there

176
00:13:04,751 --> 00:13:07,585
from his prior visit
five days earlier?

177
00:13:08,187 --> 00:13:12,147
No, as it turns out, the
door knob had been broken

178
00:13:12,325 --> 00:13:15,591
and had been changed the
morning of the day Mrs. Spalding died.

179
00:13:15,762 --> 00:13:17,697
So he had to have been
in the house that day.

180
00:13:18,631 --> 00:13:20,793
Well, what did the defendant say

181
00:13:20,967 --> 00:13:23,334
when he was confronted
with this information?

182
00:13:23,503 --> 00:13:24,698
He changed his story.

183
00:13:24,871 --> 00:13:27,152
He admitted that he had been
in the house earlier that day

184
00:13:27,307 --> 00:13:28,775
to pick up some of his clothes.

185
00:13:28,941 --> 00:13:31,934
He said that he
purposely picked that time,

186
00:13:32,111 --> 00:13:34,231
because he knew his wife
was at a doctor's appointment

187
00:13:34,380 --> 00:13:35,540
and he wouldn't run into her.

188
00:13:35,982 --> 00:13:38,144
Lieutenant, were you able
to come up with anyone

189
00:13:38,318 --> 00:13:41,482
who had seen Travis Spalding
at the house there that morning?

190
00:13:42,021 --> 00:13:44,354
- No.
- Nothing further.

191
00:13:44,524 --> 00:13:46,823
Your witness, Mr. Whiteman.

192
00:13:52,565 --> 00:13:54,193
Lieutenant.

193
00:13:56,536 --> 00:14:00,439
Can you identify this golf bag?

194
00:14:01,741 --> 00:14:03,505
Yes, that's the
defendant's golf bag.

195
00:14:03,676 --> 00:14:06,976
It's the one my men found
in Mrs. Spalding's garage.

196
00:14:07,146 --> 00:14:10,275
And down here in this
pocket, is this where you found

197
00:14:10,450 --> 00:14:14,387
Mr. Spalding's monogrammed
handkerchief soaked with ether?

198
00:14:14,554 --> 00:14:16,022
Yes, that's correct.

199
00:14:16,189 --> 00:14:20,092
Hmm. Well, tell me, lieutenant,
what prompted your men

200
00:14:20,259 --> 00:14:23,354
to look inside the pouch
of a zippered golf bag

201
00:14:23,529 --> 00:14:26,693
for a piece of evidence they
didn't even know existed?

202
00:14:26,866 --> 00:14:29,301
The pouch wasn't
completely zipped.

203
00:14:29,469 --> 00:14:32,132
The handkerchief
was partially visible.

204
00:14:32,772 --> 00:14:37,039
You mean like...?

205
00:14:37,210 --> 00:14:38,439
Like this?

206
00:14:38,611 --> 00:14:42,605
- Approximately, yes.
- Well.

207
00:14:43,449 --> 00:14:46,749
Let me see if I understand
what you're saying.

208
00:14:49,055 --> 00:14:52,787
Mr. Spalding killed his wife,

209
00:14:52,959 --> 00:14:55,190
then ran down the back
stairs into his garage,

210
00:14:55,361 --> 00:14:58,024
where for some
inexplicable reason,

211
00:14:58,197 --> 00:15:00,530
he feels the need to
hide the handkerchief

212
00:15:00,700 --> 00:15:03,169
with which he supposedly
drugged his wife.

213
00:15:03,336 --> 00:15:08,104
He then puts his monogrammed
handkerchief into his own golf bag,

214
00:15:08,274 --> 00:15:11,176
and not only forgets
to zip the bag up,

215
00:15:11,344 --> 00:15:14,212
but leaves the white
handkerchief partially exposed

216
00:15:14,380 --> 00:15:16,406
for all the world to view.

217
00:15:16,582 --> 00:15:19,984
It sort of looks like a
white flag, doesn't it?

218
00:15:20,153 --> 00:15:22,645
As if the bag
itself were yelling,

219
00:15:22,822 --> 00:15:26,088
"Hey, lieutenant, over
here! Check this out!"

220
00:15:26,259 --> 00:15:27,750
Objection.

221
00:15:27,927 --> 00:15:29,862
Sustained. Mr. Whiteman.

222
00:15:30,029 --> 00:15:34,626
I'm sorry, Your Honor,
but be honest, lieutenant.

223
00:15:34,801 --> 00:15:38,966
Doesn't this smack just a
little of a textbook frame?

224
00:15:39,939 --> 00:15:41,999
- No.
- Oh, come on.

225
00:15:42,408 --> 00:15:45,845
That golf bag and handkerchief
were not sitting in plain view

226
00:15:46,012 --> 00:15:50,211
for my men to find, as you are
leading these people to believe.

227
00:15:50,383 --> 00:15:54,286
The truth is that golf bag
was found by my men,

228
00:15:54,454 --> 00:15:56,286
hidden in the back of
a closet in the garage.

229
00:15:56,456 --> 00:15:59,517
Oh, I see. Your men
just happened on it.

230
00:15:59,692 --> 00:16:02,389
After a thorough
search of the garage.

231
00:16:02,562 --> 00:16:05,657
That's their job, Mr. Whiteman.

232
00:16:08,034 --> 00:16:09,935
Ladies and
gentlemen of the jury,

233
00:16:10,102 --> 00:16:14,540
it is now my duty to instruct you
in the law as it pertains to this case.

234
00:16:15,508 --> 00:16:18,535
It is your duty
to follow the law.

235
00:16:18,711 --> 00:16:23,979
As jurors, it is your duty to determine
the effect and value of the evidence,

236
00:16:24,150 --> 00:16:27,348
and to decide all
questions of fact.

237
00:16:27,520 --> 00:16:33,858
You must not be influenced by
sympathy, prejudice, or passion.

238
00:16:34,026 --> 00:16:37,861
The defendant is innocent
unless and until he is proven guilty

239
00:16:38,030 --> 00:16:40,329
beyond a reasonable doubt.

240
00:16:43,302 --> 00:16:47,171
<i>All 12 of you must
agree upon a verdict.</i>

241
00:16:47,340 --> 00:16:50,276
<i>Once you have reached
a verdict, signal the bailiff</i>

242
00:16:50,443 --> 00:16:54,574
<i>and the court will be
reassembled to hear your decision.</i>

243
00:16:54,747 --> 00:16:58,013
<i>The case is now in your hands.</i>

244
00:17:09,862 --> 00:17:11,990
So you're an actor?

245
00:17:12,164 --> 00:17:14,429
Yeah. You recognize me?

246
00:17:14,600 --> 00:17:17,263
No, you told the attorney
when he was questioning you.

247
00:17:17,436 --> 00:17:19,962
You're Terry the Tuna.

248
00:17:20,139 --> 00:17:22,199
Terry the Tuna?

249
00:17:22,375 --> 00:17:25,903
Wow! A celebrity.

250
00:17:26,078 --> 00:17:28,070
Can I have your autograph?

251
00:17:33,553 --> 00:17:35,522
Hello, I'm Archie Armstrong.

252
00:17:35,688 --> 00:17:36,917
Henrietta Dorsey.

253
00:17:37,089 --> 00:17:38,887
So, what do you
think of this case?

254
00:17:39,058 --> 00:17:42,222
Oh, I think we may be
out of here pretty fast.

255
00:17:42,395 --> 00:17:45,661
That Spalding guy sure had a
smug look on his face, didn't he?

256
00:17:45,831 --> 00:17:48,357
We'll see him having a
couple of beers with the guys.

257
00:17:48,534 --> 00:17:50,127
- Larry Cooper.
- Jason Brady.

258
00:17:50,303 --> 00:17:51,896
Good to meet you.

259
00:17:55,274 --> 00:17:57,038
So you're Ben Matlock, huh?

260
00:17:57,209 --> 00:17:58,802
Yeah, hi.

261
00:17:58,978 --> 00:18:00,674
Lou Green.

262
00:18:01,180 --> 00:18:03,649
So, what do you
think of the case?

263
00:18:03,816 --> 00:18:06,285
Oh, it's interesting.

264
00:18:06,452 --> 00:18:09,149
That's all? Interesting?

265
00:18:09,488 --> 00:18:12,117
Maybe we should take our seats.

266
00:18:16,228 --> 00:18:19,721
So, uh, what should we do first?

267
00:18:20,299 --> 00:18:25,203
- Maybe we should elect a foreman.
- Yeah.

268
00:18:25,371 --> 00:18:27,340
Any volunteers?

269
00:18:27,807 --> 00:18:31,073
What about you? You're
sitting at the head of the table.

270
00:18:31,243 --> 00:18:33,303
I'd be very happy
to be foreperson.

271
00:18:33,479 --> 00:18:35,107
I think we should go with him.

272
00:18:35,281 --> 00:18:38,445
Well, why? I mean, you
don't know either one of us.

273
00:18:38,618 --> 00:18:40,086
Is it because he's a man?

274
00:18:41,454 --> 00:18:43,446
What, are you some
kind of a feminist?

275
00:18:44,690 --> 00:18:46,090
It's okay. Forget it.

276
00:18:48,594 --> 00:18:54,556
I anoint you chief
executioner. Ha-ha-ha.

277
00:19:01,173 --> 00:19:03,108
Well, uh...

278
00:19:03,509 --> 00:19:05,740
I'm really not sure
what to do. Um...

279
00:19:05,911 --> 00:19:07,911
I guess we could just
start talking about the case,

280
00:19:08,080 --> 00:19:09,981
or if you like, we could
take a preliminary vote

281
00:19:10,149 --> 00:19:12,084
- and see where we stand.
- I say we vote.

282
00:19:12,885 --> 00:19:14,945
There might not be
anything to talk about.

283
00:19:15,521 --> 00:19:18,923
All right, unless anybody objects,
why don't we do it by a show of hands?

284
00:19:19,091 --> 00:19:20,992
All those in favor
of voting guilty.

285
00:19:29,168 --> 00:19:32,002
Those in favor of
voting not-guilty?

286
00:19:34,774 --> 00:19:36,675
Ugh. I don't believe this.

287
00:19:36,842 --> 00:19:38,276
You're kidding, aren't you?

288
00:19:39,378 --> 00:19:42,405
- Eleven to one.
- What's the problem?

289
00:19:42,915 --> 00:19:45,043
The evidence against Travis
Spalding's pretty strong.

290
00:19:45,217 --> 00:19:48,085
Strong? He lied to the police
about being in the house,

291
00:19:48,254 --> 00:19:51,452
and his fingerprints were
there. What else would you like?

292
00:19:51,624 --> 00:19:53,320
Do you honestly
believe he's not guilty?

293
00:19:53,492 --> 00:19:56,621
No. He may very
well have done it.

294
00:19:56,796 --> 00:20:00,597
It's just that there are a couple
of things I'm confused about.

295
00:20:00,766 --> 00:20:03,065
They should have never
put a lawyer on the jury.

296
00:20:03,235 --> 00:20:05,101
What are your
questions, Mr. Matlock?

297
00:20:05,871 --> 00:20:07,863
Um... Uh...

298
00:20:09,141 --> 00:20:13,044
The police think
that Travis Spalding

299
00:20:15,214 --> 00:20:17,979
entered his wife's
house through the garage

300
00:20:18,818 --> 00:20:22,983
and went upstairs and poured
ether on his handkerchief.

301
00:20:23,155 --> 00:20:25,075
Wait, you're not gonna
start with the thing about

302
00:20:25,224 --> 00:20:26,852
"why would he use
his own handkerchief"?

303
00:20:27,026 --> 00:20:28,790
No, no, no.

304
00:20:28,961 --> 00:20:32,090
And they think that
he held the ether

305
00:20:32,264 --> 00:20:35,860
over his wife's mouth
and nose until he killed her.

306
00:20:36,035 --> 00:20:39,904
Then he heard the maid
and went back downstairs

307
00:20:40,072 --> 00:20:42,564
and hid the handkerchief
in his golf bag.

308
00:20:42,742 --> 00:20:45,610
- So, what's the problem?
- Why'd he hide the handkerchief?

309
00:20:45,778 --> 00:20:48,907
I can answer that. Because he
was in a hurry to get out of the house

310
00:20:49,081 --> 00:20:52,313
and maybe he was worried
that the maid knew it was him.

311
00:20:52,485 --> 00:20:54,852
And I'm sure he didn't want to
have the handkerchief on him,

312
00:20:55,020 --> 00:20:56,980
in case the police picked him up.
- Yeah.

313
00:20:57,156 --> 00:21:02,254
But why would he hide the
handkerchief at the scene of the murder

314
00:21:02,428 --> 00:21:06,229
when he knew the police were
gonna search that house for evidence?

315
00:21:06,398 --> 00:21:09,493
Actually, I have to admit that that
never made a lot of sense to me either.

316
00:21:09,668 --> 00:21:12,160
It'd be the last place I'd hide it.
- And the ether.

317
00:21:12,338 --> 00:21:14,307
I mean, why did he
hide the handkerchief

318
00:21:14,473 --> 00:21:15,873
and take the ether with him?

319
00:21:16,041 --> 00:21:19,205
That's why you're keeping us
here, because of a bottle of ether?

320
00:21:19,378 --> 00:21:21,176
Well, maybe it's
still in the house.

321
00:21:21,781 --> 00:21:23,875
Did the police search
the house for the bottle?

322
00:21:24,483 --> 00:21:27,851
No, he's right. I mean, the
easy thing, the logical thing

323
00:21:28,020 --> 00:21:31,320
is to take both the handkerchief
and the bottle out of the house,

324
00:21:31,490 --> 00:21:33,083
or leave them both there.

325
00:21:33,259 --> 00:21:35,990
I mean, it doesn't make any sense
to take one and not take the other.

326
00:21:36,162 --> 00:21:38,927
Maybe he put the ether on the
handkerchief outside the house,

327
00:21:39,098 --> 00:21:41,966
- and then just left the bottle out there.
- Yeah, how about that?

328
00:21:42,134 --> 00:21:45,832
Well, I say again, why would
he hide the handkerchief

329
00:21:46,005 --> 00:21:47,701
and leave the ether in the car?

330
00:21:47,873 --> 00:21:49,933
Could he have put the
ether on the handkerchief

331
00:21:50,109 --> 00:21:51,600
before he left his apartment?

332
00:21:52,044 --> 00:21:54,980
Wouldn't he worry about the
ether losing some of its potency

333
00:21:55,147 --> 00:21:56,479
on the way over to the house?

334
00:21:56,649 --> 00:21:58,174
Wait, what's wrong
with you people?

335
00:21:58,350 --> 00:22:01,980
The guy was gonna be out 10
million bucks if she divorced him.

336
00:22:02,154 --> 00:22:04,885
I mean, didn't you listen? He
told her he was gonna kill her.

337
00:22:05,057 --> 00:22:06,257
Fingerprints are at the house.

338
00:22:06,425 --> 00:22:08,425
And you people are talking
about some stupid bottle

339
00:22:08,594 --> 00:22:10,586
that nobody gives a
damn about. I mean...

340
00:22:10,763 --> 00:22:12,994
I'm sorry, I do give
a damn about it.

341
00:22:13,165 --> 00:22:15,760
- I give a damn too.
- Well, then you're as stupid as he is.

342
00:22:15,935 --> 00:22:18,837
You know what? I'm getting tired
of the way that you talk to people.

343
00:22:19,004 --> 00:22:20,905
Okay. All right, people.
Hey, hey, hey, come on.

344
00:22:21,073 --> 00:22:23,406
Hey, let's just settle down.

345
00:22:23,576 --> 00:22:26,068
I really think you guys are
focusing on the small stuff.

346
00:22:26,245 --> 00:22:29,306
I mean, think about the
evidence against this guy.

347
00:22:29,515 --> 00:22:31,677
- Hallelujah.
- Listen, there are a lot of people here

348
00:22:31,851 --> 00:22:34,844
who haven't voiced their opinion,
and I think we should hear from them.

349
00:22:35,020 --> 00:22:36,079
How about you, Henrietta?

350
00:22:37,523 --> 00:22:40,015
Oh, I think the man did it.

351
00:22:40,192 --> 00:22:42,821
I guess I don't care very
much about this bottle thing,

352
00:22:42,995 --> 00:22:45,794
or what he should've
done if he was smarter.

353
00:22:45,965 --> 00:22:51,700
Fact is, he was there. And
he lied about being there.

354
00:22:54,073 --> 00:22:55,405
What about you, Lionel?

355
00:22:56,742 --> 00:23:01,544
Well, um, didn't he say he'd
kill her if she divorced him?

356
00:23:01,714 --> 00:23:04,183
That's exactly what he said.
- Oh, come on.

357
00:23:04,350 --> 00:23:08,287
We've all said, "I'll kill you for
that," or, "I'll kill you if you're late."

358
00:23:08,454 --> 00:23:12,221
You heard what the lawyer said. If
that was a crime, we'd all be in jail.

359
00:23:12,391 --> 00:23:14,053
I know that.

360
00:23:14,226 --> 00:23:18,891
It's just that, well, he was
gonna lose $10 million.

361
00:23:19,064 --> 00:23:20,088
Jennifer?

362
00:23:21,700 --> 00:23:24,033
Do we know if Travis
Spalding has a criminal record?

363
00:23:24,203 --> 00:23:29,039
We can't consider that. It wasn't
part of what was said in court.

364
00:23:29,208 --> 00:23:32,007
No one asked for your
legal opinion, Mr. Matlock.

365
00:23:32,177 --> 00:23:35,773
I was just trying to tell you
that we can't consider it.

366
00:23:35,948 --> 00:23:38,850
Well, just, why don't you keep your
mouth shut and let other people talk?

367
00:23:39,018 --> 00:23:41,578
Look, there's no reason to be rude.
- No. Listen to me.

368
00:23:42,788 --> 00:23:47,089
Rich punks like Travis Spalding,
they think they're above the law.

369
00:23:47,259 --> 00:23:50,229
Well, not here, not now.

370
00:23:50,396 --> 00:23:53,889
I see guys like him at
my garage every day.

371
00:23:54,066 --> 00:23:57,036
They drive in, in
their big fancy cars.

372
00:23:57,202 --> 00:24:00,001
Talk to me like I don't exist.

373
00:24:01,974 --> 00:24:04,136
That doesn't mean he's guilty.

374
00:24:04,777 --> 00:24:06,905
I think we should
take another vote.

375
00:24:07,579 --> 00:24:11,277
Fine. Anybody have
a problem with that?

376
00:24:12,351 --> 00:24:15,549
Maybe this time we
should write our votes down.

377
00:24:15,721 --> 00:24:18,316
Why? People should have the
guts to stand by their decision.

378
00:24:18,490 --> 00:24:22,052
People should have the
right to vote the way they feel

379
00:24:22,227 --> 00:24:26,631
without worrying about
having their heads bitten off.

380
00:24:26,799 --> 00:24:29,268
Fine. What do I care?

381
00:24:31,637 --> 00:24:32,969
Guilty.

382
00:24:34,239 --> 00:24:35,901
Not guilty.

383
00:24:36,575 --> 00:24:37,941
Guilty.

384
00:24:38,777 --> 00:24:40,109
Guilty.

385
00:24:41,680 --> 00:24:43,012
Guilty.

386
00:24:44,016 --> 00:24:45,279
Not guilty.

387
00:24:45,451 --> 00:24:49,149
That's nine guilties
and three not-guilties.

388
00:24:49,321 --> 00:24:50,721
Well, you know what they say:

389
00:24:50,856 --> 00:24:54,224
"It's a woman's prerogative
to change her mind."

390
00:24:54,626 --> 00:24:56,219
Don't you dare
talk to me like that.

391
00:24:56,395 --> 00:24:59,058
All right, fess up. Who was the
other person who voted not-guilty?

392
00:24:59,231 --> 00:25:01,598
You don't have to answer that.

393
00:25:03,168 --> 00:25:04,466
It was me.

394
00:25:06,005 --> 00:25:08,031
Well, I should
have figured that.

395
00:25:10,242 --> 00:25:12,711
- What does that mean?
- Forget it.

396
00:25:13,312 --> 00:25:14,712
I wanna know what
you meant by that.

397
00:25:14,847 --> 00:25:16,748
Ramon, it isn't worth it.

398
00:25:16,915 --> 00:25:18,474
I wanna know what he meant.

399
00:25:18,650 --> 00:25:21,415
Half your cousins
are probably in jail.

400
00:25:21,587 --> 00:25:25,285
You and your people, this
country opens its arms to you.

401
00:25:26,025 --> 00:25:27,985
It doesn't matter that you
crawl across the border

402
00:25:28,127 --> 00:25:30,323
and steal real jobs
from real Americans.

403
00:25:30,496 --> 00:25:32,795
Real Americans? Heh.

404
00:25:34,066 --> 00:25:36,228
I'm a United States citizen.

405
00:25:36,402 --> 00:25:39,736
I pay taxes just like
anybody else in this room.

406
00:25:39,905 --> 00:25:41,203
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

407
00:25:41,373 --> 00:25:42,807
Don't talk down to me, man.

408
00:25:44,777 --> 00:25:46,939
You sit there, pretending
to be one of us.

409
00:25:47,112 --> 00:25:49,138
I got news for you, amigo.

410
00:25:49,314 --> 00:25:52,148
You never will be. You'll
never even get close.

411
00:25:52,317 --> 00:25:54,786
- Hey, hey, come on.
- Guys.

412
00:25:56,455 --> 00:25:58,583
They're real Americans!
They're real Americans!

413
00:25:58,757 --> 00:26:01,283
They're real Americans!

414
00:26:16,842 --> 00:26:19,607
I think we should tell the
judge what's going on in here.

415
00:26:19,778 --> 00:26:22,247
- What's he gonna do?
- I don't know.

416
00:26:22,414 --> 00:26:25,816
You can't tell me this is
what happens on most juries.

417
00:26:25,984 --> 00:26:27,885
Why don't we just tell
him we're a hung jury?

418
00:26:28,053 --> 00:26:30,045
We are not a hung
jury unless we give up.

419
00:26:30,222 --> 00:26:33,784
Now, we just can't throw in the towel
because we disagree with each other.

420
00:26:33,959 --> 00:26:37,020
Nobody said that this
was going to be easy.

421
00:26:37,196 --> 00:26:39,825
Hey, Ben, what do you think?

422
00:26:41,667 --> 00:26:44,466
- Why don't we break for lunch?
- What?

423
00:26:44,636 --> 00:26:46,901
Well, aren't you hungry?

424
00:26:47,072 --> 00:26:49,541
If we eat, maybe
we'll cool down.

425
00:26:49,942 --> 00:26:53,003
- I second the motion.
- Is that okay with everybody else?

426
00:26:53,178 --> 00:26:54,646
Yes.

427
00:26:54,813 --> 00:26:56,975
So, what do we order?

428
00:27:08,660 --> 00:27:11,824
- You work here in town?
- Yeah, phone company.

429
00:27:11,997 --> 00:27:15,559
Well, I used to. I'm
in between jobs now.

430
00:27:15,734 --> 00:27:16,963
Oh.

431
00:27:17,136 --> 00:27:19,105
- You?
- Interior design.

432
00:27:19,271 --> 00:27:20,637
Very good.

433
00:27:20,806 --> 00:27:24,436
Wow. What a coincidence.

434
00:27:25,277 --> 00:27:27,712
I drink water too.

435
00:27:29,481 --> 00:27:31,245
You gonna eat that pickle?

436
00:27:31,416 --> 00:27:33,385
No, it's all yours.

437
00:27:33,919 --> 00:27:35,353
You know, it's none
of my beeswax,

438
00:27:35,521 --> 00:27:37,717
but you might wanna
watch your salt intake.

439
00:27:37,890 --> 00:27:40,018
You sound like my daughter.

440
00:27:40,425 --> 00:27:43,827
- I do 40 push-ups every morning.
- Doggone.

441
00:27:44,296 --> 00:27:46,288
Course, I need an
oxygen tent when I'm done.

442
00:27:48,433 --> 00:27:50,629
Ramon. Ramon.

443
00:27:50,802 --> 00:27:54,170
- Hey, you okay?
- Yeah, yeah.

444
00:27:54,907 --> 00:27:57,502
I'm sorry about
the fight, you know?

445
00:27:58,010 --> 00:27:59,330
Hey, you don't
have to apologize.

446
00:27:59,478 --> 00:28:01,318
I don't think I would've
acted any differently.

447
00:28:01,680 --> 00:28:04,844
No, but he's just an
angry man, you know?

448
00:28:05,017 --> 00:28:07,612
He sure is. Hey, hey, listen.

449
00:28:07,786 --> 00:28:10,221
You think you're ready
to give it another shot?

450
00:28:10,656 --> 00:28:12,488
- Yeah.
- Good.

451
00:28:12,658 --> 00:28:15,025
Okay, people, let's go to work.

452
00:28:17,196 --> 00:28:19,688
Folks, folks. Everybody,
please, please.

453
00:28:19,865 --> 00:28:22,630
Come on, let's get back to
the housekeeper's testimony.

454
00:28:22,801 --> 00:28:24,201
What did we get from her?

455
00:28:24,369 --> 00:28:25,837
We get that Lenore Spalding

456
00:28:26,004 --> 00:28:27,905
couldn't stand the
sight of her own cooking.

457
00:28:30,242 --> 00:28:32,711
Don't...? No, don't you
remember? This isn't a joke.

458
00:28:32,878 --> 00:28:35,609
The maid said that Lenore
Spalding made herself an entire meal,

459
00:28:35,781 --> 00:28:37,841
but she didn't eat a bite of it.

460
00:28:38,016 --> 00:28:41,578
She couldn't stand the
sight of her own cooking.

461
00:28:42,354 --> 00:28:46,155
You make jokes, but
when you think of it,

462
00:28:46,325 --> 00:28:51,320
this weak woman,
hurting from a bad back,

463
00:28:51,496 --> 00:28:54,955
took all the pain
and effort to walk...

464
00:28:55,133 --> 00:28:59,764
What did they say?
- -twenty-three steps downstairs

465
00:28:59,938 --> 00:29:02,203
to fix herself supper

466
00:29:02,374 --> 00:29:05,970
and then just leaves
it there on the table

467
00:29:06,144 --> 00:29:08,545
and goes back upstairs to sleep.

468
00:29:08,714 --> 00:29:10,074
This is worth a lot of time.

469
00:29:10,215 --> 00:29:11,979
Maybe we should spend
a couple of days here

470
00:29:12,150 --> 00:29:14,278
to talk about Lenore
Spalding's eating habits.

471
00:29:14,453 --> 00:29:17,821
Son, I wasn't rude to
you when you spoke.

472
00:29:17,990 --> 00:29:20,687
I'm not asking you
to agree with me,

473
00:29:20,859 --> 00:29:26,355
but I am asking you for what we've
all been decent enough to give you:

474
00:29:26,531 --> 00:29:28,432
a little respect.

475
00:29:32,771 --> 00:29:35,570
Henrietta, my guess is
that Lenore Spalding felt sick

476
00:29:35,741 --> 00:29:36,936
when she got downstairs,

477
00:29:37,109 --> 00:29:39,510
lost her appetite, and
decided to go back to her room.

478
00:29:39,678 --> 00:29:44,309
It's odd though. She had the
strength to walk up 20-some steps,

479
00:29:44,483 --> 00:29:47,942
but she didn't have it in her to
put the milk back in the icebox.

480
00:29:48,120 --> 00:29:51,818
May I remind you, the
woman was worth $20 million.

481
00:29:51,990 --> 00:29:54,391
Do you think she cared
about a carton of milk?

482
00:29:54,559 --> 00:29:56,960
You know, there is
another explanation for this.

483
00:29:57,129 --> 00:30:00,930
But what if Lenore Spalding wasn't
drugged in her bed like the police said?

484
00:30:01,099 --> 00:30:05,161
I mean, what if she made herself dinner
and was drugged before she ever ate,

485
00:30:05,337 --> 00:30:08,535
and was then dragged
upstairs and killed?

486
00:30:08,707 --> 00:30:10,676
- I don't believe this.
- Well, why not?

487
00:30:10,842 --> 00:30:13,710
The police didn't find a trace of
ether on her bed or on her pillow.

488
00:30:13,879 --> 00:30:15,939
It would explain why
the food wasn't eaten.

489
00:30:16,114 --> 00:30:19,050
They didn't find any ether
in the dining room either.

490
00:30:19,217 --> 00:30:22,051
You people are
losing your minds here.

491
00:30:22,220 --> 00:30:24,660
You're wasting all this time
talking about how she was killed,

492
00:30:24,790 --> 00:30:28,591
where she was drugged, why
she didn't eat. It doesn't matter.

493
00:30:29,494 --> 00:30:33,829
What matters is who.
And we know who.

494
00:30:36,001 --> 00:30:38,698
Okay, come on.
Let's just move on.

495
00:30:38,870 --> 00:30:44,400
Basically didn't the housekeeper just
testify about Travis Spalding's motive?

496
00:30:44,576 --> 00:30:47,546
It's not like she saw
or she heard anything

497
00:30:47,713 --> 00:30:50,239
that pointed to him
as the murderer.

498
00:30:51,083 --> 00:30:53,450
Did the housekeeper say she
parked in front of the house?

499
00:30:53,618 --> 00:30:55,246
- Yeah.
- Well, why do you suppose

500
00:30:55,420 --> 00:30:59,050
she didn't mention seeing Travis
Spalding's car parked out there?

501
00:30:59,958 --> 00:31:01,688
He was committing
a murder inside.

502
00:31:01,860 --> 00:31:04,141
He would have been an idiot
to park in front of the house.

503
00:31:04,296 --> 00:31:06,456
Yeah, I always figured he
parked the car in the garage,

504
00:31:06,598 --> 00:31:09,397
and decided to close
it from the inside.

505
00:31:09,568 --> 00:31:11,002
What kind of car did he drive?

506
00:31:11,169 --> 00:31:15,038
A Testarossa. It's Italian.
Tom Cruise has one, I think.

507
00:31:15,207 --> 00:31:17,176
It's a Ferrari.

508
00:31:17,442 --> 00:31:18,466
What kind of engine?

509
00:31:18,643 --> 00:31:23,081
Twelve cylinder, 423
horsepower. What's that matter?

510
00:31:23,548 --> 00:31:25,312
Must have made a bunch a racket.

511
00:31:25,851 --> 00:31:27,217
So?

512
00:31:27,386 --> 00:31:30,550
Especially if it was
parked in a closed garage.

513
00:31:30,722 --> 00:31:34,352
Don't you see? It would've sounded
like an echo chamber in there.

514
00:31:34,526 --> 00:31:36,757
If Travis Spalding
started the car,

515
00:31:36,928 --> 00:31:39,295
the maid would have
recognized the sound immediately.

516
00:31:39,464 --> 00:31:43,697
But she said she didn't
hear anything but footsteps.

517
00:31:43,869 --> 00:31:46,100
Maybe he didn't use his own car.

518
00:31:46,938 --> 00:31:50,739
Did any of you consider the possibility
that maybe he didn't park in his garage?

519
00:31:50,909 --> 00:31:53,845
Maybe he parked a block
away. Maybe he took a bus.

520
00:31:54,012 --> 00:31:55,310
That's certainly possible.

521
00:31:55,781 --> 00:31:58,683
I wanna know why you people
are trying to let this rich boy off.

522
00:31:58,850 --> 00:32:01,342
We're just considering
the evidence, Larry.

523
00:32:01,520 --> 00:32:04,600
Give me a break. The facts in this case
are as plain as the nose on your face.

524
00:32:04,756 --> 00:32:06,816
He had motive,
he had opportunity.

525
00:32:06,992 --> 00:32:09,621
Fingerprints were there. He
knew it was the maid's day off.

526
00:32:09,795 --> 00:32:11,696
I don't care where he
parked or what he drove.

527
00:32:11,863 --> 00:32:16,767
I don't know you, but I'll bet that if it
was your life we were talking about,

528
00:32:16,935 --> 00:32:22,203
you'd be out there praying that we'd
consider every angle, every possibility.

529
00:32:22,374 --> 00:32:24,934
Well, you're just trying to twist
things so you can let him walk.

530
00:32:25,110 --> 00:32:27,807
The maid didn't see him.
The maid didn't hear him.

531
00:32:27,979 --> 00:32:30,778
The maid didn't hear his car.

532
00:32:31,216 --> 00:32:33,185
We don't even know
if he was in the house

533
00:32:33,351 --> 00:32:35,149
when this murder was committed.

534
00:32:35,320 --> 00:32:37,755
His fingerprints
were on the door.

535
00:32:37,923 --> 00:32:40,449
That could've
happened at any time.

536
00:32:40,625 --> 00:32:43,493
- Then why did he lie?
- Because he was scared.

537
00:32:43,662 --> 00:32:45,528
He knew he was
suspected of murder.

538
00:32:45,697 --> 00:32:49,464
Does that lie prove
beyond a reasonable doubt

539
00:32:49,634 --> 00:32:52,570
that this man
committed this murder?

540
00:32:52,737 --> 00:32:56,333
May I interrupt? It's been a while since
we've taken a vote. Anybody object?

541
00:32:56,508 --> 00:32:58,670
I say we do it by show of hands.

542
00:32:58,844 --> 00:33:01,109
- Anybody have a problem
with that? ALL: Not at all. No.

543
00:33:01,279 --> 00:33:04,579
All those in favor
of a guilty verdict.

544
00:33:05,817 --> 00:33:08,753
One, two, three,
four, five, six. Okay.

545
00:33:08,920 --> 00:33:11,549
Those in favor of
a not-guilty verdict.

546
00:33:11,723 --> 00:33:14,955
One, two, three,
four, five, six.

547
00:33:15,126 --> 00:33:18,221
Six guilty, six not-guilty.

548
00:33:18,396 --> 00:33:19,887
You happy now?

549
00:33:20,065 --> 00:33:23,160
Hey, Ben, the guy is
a sucker for a left hook.

550
00:33:23,335 --> 00:33:24,826
Will you shut up?

551
00:33:28,173 --> 00:33:31,439
You think I'm trying to get
the defendant off. I'm not.

552
00:33:31,610 --> 00:33:34,170
Well, as Joe Friday used to
say, "Let's look at the facts."

553
00:33:34,346 --> 00:33:37,612
We started with one not-guilty,
now there are six, thanks to you.

554
00:33:37,782 --> 00:33:42,720
All I hope is that each person
will vote the way they believe.

555
00:33:42,888 --> 00:33:47,087
No, what you hope is that each
person will vote the way you believe.

556
00:33:47,259 --> 00:33:50,339
I'll give you credit. You're pretty smart.
You're probably a very good lawyer,

557
00:33:50,495 --> 00:33:54,660
the way you poked holes in all
this evidence, but I think he did it.

558
00:33:54,833 --> 00:33:58,065
And there's really nothing
you can do to change my mind.

559
00:34:16,922 --> 00:34:20,791
- How long do juries usually stay out?
- Depends.

560
00:34:20,959 --> 00:34:25,761
Capital cases take a long time. I
once waited for a verdict for 11 days.

561
00:34:25,931 --> 00:34:29,129
They finish when they finish.

562
00:34:29,301 --> 00:34:31,236
- Eleven days?
- Mm-hm.

563
00:34:31,403 --> 00:34:34,896
I say we call it quits. Tell
the judge we're deadlocked.

564
00:34:35,073 --> 00:34:36,564
Got a hot date?

565
00:34:36,741 --> 00:34:38,903
No, I don't have a hot date.

566
00:34:39,077 --> 00:34:40,797
But let's get real.
We've been here all day,

567
00:34:40,946 --> 00:34:43,415
and we're further away
than we were before.

568
00:34:43,582 --> 00:34:47,041
Hey, I hope you people
understand why I voted guilty.

569
00:34:47,218 --> 00:34:50,916
I don't mean to be difficult, but I just
don't see enough reasonable doubt.

570
00:34:51,089 --> 00:34:53,490
You don't have to
apologize to anybody.

571
00:34:53,658 --> 00:34:57,595
I know. I guess I feel guilty agreeing
with anything Larry Cooper says.

572
00:35:02,567 --> 00:35:06,231
Well, you know what they say:
"Rich men don't go to jail for murder."

573
00:35:06,404 --> 00:35:08,498
Now I know why.

574
00:35:08,673 --> 00:35:11,142
You think this Ben Matlock
really cares if the guy's rich?

575
00:35:11,309 --> 00:35:12,834
I don't know.

576
00:35:13,445 --> 00:35:17,849
But these defense attorneys, they'll
do anything to get their clients off.

577
00:35:18,016 --> 00:35:19,917
It's not his client.

578
00:35:22,687 --> 00:35:24,315
You think Ben Matlock's right?

579
00:35:24,823 --> 00:35:26,155
No.

580
00:35:26,891 --> 00:35:29,087
But he seems honest.

581
00:35:31,062 --> 00:35:33,429
Come on. We should go back.

582
00:35:34,065 --> 00:35:35,089
Yeah.

583
00:35:35,266 --> 00:35:36,825
Oh, boy.

584
00:35:38,069 --> 00:35:41,267
- Almost forgot to take my vitamins.
- Yeah.

585
00:35:42,941 --> 00:35:44,910
What's the program?

586
00:35:45,076 --> 00:35:47,136
Well, we were
discussing calling the bailiff

587
00:35:47,312 --> 00:35:49,472
and having him tell the
judge that we're at an impasse.

588
00:35:49,614 --> 00:35:52,083
- I say do it.
- We've only been at it six hours.

589
00:35:52,250 --> 00:35:54,719
Well, it sounds like
a good idea to me.

590
00:35:55,320 --> 00:35:57,380
Will you look at that?

591
00:35:58,590 --> 00:36:02,721
- Oh, here, Mr. Green.
- It's okay, I got it. I got it.

592
00:36:05,096 --> 00:36:08,464
Uh, could I see the pictures
of the murder scene?

593
00:36:08,833 --> 00:36:11,359
Oh, what now?

594
00:36:16,708 --> 00:36:20,440
Jason, you believe Travis
Spalding killed his wife.

595
00:36:20,612 --> 00:36:23,411
- Yeah, I do.
- Okay. How'd he do it?

596
00:36:23,915 --> 00:36:26,976
- How'd he do it?
- Yeah, how'd he do it?

597
00:36:27,786 --> 00:36:29,049
Like the police said.

598
00:36:29,220 --> 00:36:32,418
She was in bed, he covered her
mouth with ether to knock her out,

599
00:36:32,590 --> 00:36:35,685
and then he took
her to the bathroom

600
00:36:35,860 --> 00:36:38,523
and cracked her head
on the tile bathtub steps.

601
00:36:38,697 --> 00:36:40,495
How'd he get her
to the bathroom?

602
00:36:42,467 --> 00:36:44,231
I don't know.

603
00:36:45,470 --> 00:36:46,802
Well, he probably dragged her.

604
00:36:47,572 --> 00:36:49,268
These pictures were
taken by the police...

605
00:36:49,441 --> 00:36:52,639
This is Mrs. Spalding's
bedroom, you know?

606
00:36:52,811 --> 00:36:55,440
That they took just after they
found her dead, right, right?

607
00:36:55,613 --> 00:36:57,809
- Right.
- Okay.

608
00:36:57,982 --> 00:37:01,919
There are no rug
marks on the carpet.

609
00:37:02,087 --> 00:37:04,113
- What does that mean?
- Rug marks.

610
00:37:04,289 --> 00:37:07,817
If he dragged his wife from
the bedroom to the bathroom

611
00:37:07,992 --> 00:37:09,620
across this thick carpet,

612
00:37:09,794 --> 00:37:13,162
shouldn't there be rug marks
where her feet dragged?

613
00:37:13,331 --> 00:37:14,697
No, no. Come on. Wait.

614
00:37:14,866 --> 00:37:18,428
Isn't it possible that Travis
Spalding put a gun to his wife's head

615
00:37:18,603 --> 00:37:20,333
and forced her
into the bathroom?

616
00:37:20,505 --> 00:37:23,475
The maid said Mrs. Spalding
couldn't walk without her cane.

617
00:37:23,641 --> 00:37:25,371
Where's her cane?

618
00:37:29,380 --> 00:37:30,814
Next to her bed.

619
00:37:33,551 --> 00:37:35,110
All right, he carried her.

620
00:37:35,286 --> 00:37:40,623
Larry, would you be willing
to be part of a demonstration?

621
00:37:41,059 --> 00:37:43,460
- What do you mean a demonstration?
- A demonstration.

622
00:37:43,628 --> 00:37:47,065
I'll make a deal with
you. You give me this.

623
00:37:47,232 --> 00:37:50,862
And if some of you still
believe this man killed his wife,

624
00:37:51,035 --> 00:37:53,834
then I'll tell the bailiff
we're a hung jury myself.

625
00:37:54,005 --> 00:37:55,337
I think that's fair, Larry.

626
00:37:55,507 --> 00:37:57,408
- Very fair.
- That's right.

627
00:37:59,978 --> 00:38:01,139
Fine. MATLOCK: Good.

628
00:38:01,546 --> 00:38:04,072
Now, how much would you
say that Mr. Spalding weighed?

629
00:38:04,249 --> 00:38:05,877
A hundred and forty, 145 pounds?

630
00:38:06,050 --> 00:38:07,530
Something like that. - Mid-40s.

631
00:38:07,685 --> 00:38:10,052
- What do you weigh, Larry?
- I'm 155.

632
00:38:10,221 --> 00:38:12,554
Perfect. We know from
the medical examiner that

633
00:38:12,724 --> 00:38:15,421
Mrs. Spalding weighed a hundred...
- A hundred and eighty.

634
00:38:15,593 --> 00:38:17,553
And eighty pounds. A
hundred and eighty pounds.

635
00:38:17,695 --> 00:38:20,597
Uh, uh, ahem.

636
00:38:20,765 --> 00:38:24,566
Henrietta, roughly how much...?

637
00:38:24,736 --> 00:38:26,398
In your dreams, honey.

638
00:38:28,840 --> 00:38:30,069
Am I in the ballpark?

639
00:38:31,609 --> 00:38:33,805
I'm afraid so.

640
00:38:33,978 --> 00:38:36,345
Would you be part
of the demonstration?

641
00:38:37,215 --> 00:38:38,581
Doing what?

642
00:38:38,750 --> 00:38:41,948
Just getting up on this table.

643
00:38:42,654 --> 00:38:47,183
Ten dollars a day, 15 cents a mile,
and that grin you got on your face

644
00:38:47,358 --> 00:38:50,123
ain't enough to get
me up on this table.

645
00:38:50,295 --> 00:38:53,993
How about please, Henrietta?

646
00:38:54,165 --> 00:38:57,067
- Please. Please?
- Mm-mm.

647
00:38:59,337 --> 00:39:00,498
Okay, thanks.

648
00:39:00,672 --> 00:39:03,801
There. Okay, now you go
ahead. Here you just... Here we go.

649
00:39:03,975 --> 00:39:05,603
- I'll help you.
- All right. Thank you.

650
00:39:05,777 --> 00:39:08,303
Now just get up there,
on your back, face up.

651
00:39:08,479 --> 00:39:09,572
Oh, this is embarrassing.

652
00:39:09,747 --> 00:39:11,909
It'll only take a minute.
Only take a minute.

653
00:39:12,083 --> 00:39:15,611
There you go. There
you go. Okay, that's...

654
00:39:15,787 --> 00:39:18,814
Oh, that's perfect for under
her head. That's right. Okay.

655
00:39:18,990 --> 00:39:20,686
- You all right?
- Yes, fine.

656
00:39:20,859 --> 00:39:24,387
There we go. All right.

657
00:39:24,562 --> 00:39:25,825
Larry, pick her up.

658
00:39:29,167 --> 00:39:30,191
Excuse me?

659
00:39:30,368 --> 00:39:33,600
Well, we know that Ms. Spalding
didn't walk in the bathroom.

660
00:39:33,771 --> 00:39:36,104
We know that her husband
didn't drag her in there.

661
00:39:36,274 --> 00:39:39,972
So that means he had to pick her
up and carry her more than 30 feet.

662
00:39:42,513 --> 00:39:43,811
This is stupid.

663
00:39:44,515 --> 00:39:46,780
Afraid you can't do it, Larry?

664
00:39:46,951 --> 00:39:48,943
Why don't you be quiet, old man?

665
00:39:49,320 --> 00:39:50,811
This isn't gonna prove anything.

666
00:39:51,756 --> 00:39:54,385
One of the first things you said
when you walked into this room

667
00:39:54,559 --> 00:39:57,495
was that you thought Mr. Spalding
hadn't sweated a day in his life.

668
00:39:57,862 --> 00:40:02,323
Well, if Mr. Spalding was
able to pick up his wife's body,

669
00:40:02,500 --> 00:40:06,767
then Larry shouldn't
have any problems doing it.

670
00:40:07,205 --> 00:40:09,367
Come on, Larry.
What's it gonna hurt?

671
00:40:09,974 --> 00:40:11,499
If you do it, we're out of here.

672
00:40:11,676 --> 00:40:14,043
- I'd like you to do it, Larry.
- So would I.

673
00:40:17,181 --> 00:40:18,877
Well...

674
00:40:23,888 --> 00:40:27,381
You drop me, boy, there's
gonna be another murder trial,

675
00:40:27,558 --> 00:40:29,652
and you get to be
the one that's dead.

676
00:40:32,931 --> 00:40:35,560
Go ahead, Larry. Pick her
up. Carry her across the room.

677
00:40:35,733 --> 00:40:37,634
Go ahead. Go ahead.

678
00:40:53,351 --> 00:40:54,546
He can't lift her.

679
00:40:54,719 --> 00:40:56,187
I don't believe this.

680
00:40:56,554 --> 00:40:57,681
You wanna give me a minute?

681
00:40:57,855 --> 00:41:00,689
- All right, come on, Larry, just...
- Take your hands off me.

682
00:41:00,858 --> 00:41:03,020
You're a strong man, Larry.

683
00:41:03,194 --> 00:41:05,595
You look a lot stronger
than Travis Spalding.

684
00:41:06,264 --> 00:41:07,425
He couldn't have done it.

685
00:41:07,598 --> 00:41:10,363
- I can do it.
- That's enough, Larry.

686
00:41:10,535 --> 00:41:13,198
You're gonna hurt
yourself and Henrietta.

687
00:41:13,371 --> 00:41:16,068
Here. There you go.

688
00:41:16,908 --> 00:41:21,812
Well, I'm convinced.
I'm changing my vote.

689
00:41:22,580 --> 00:41:24,139
Me too.

690
00:41:24,849 --> 00:41:26,215
Larry.

691
00:41:28,353 --> 00:41:29,844
He couldn't have done it.

692
00:41:34,158 --> 00:41:35,592
Well, I think he could.

693
00:41:35,760 --> 00:41:37,524
I think you're wrong.

694
00:41:38,696 --> 00:41:42,929
Larry, you don't like
me and I don't care.

695
00:41:43,101 --> 00:41:46,094
This isn't about
winning or losing.

696
00:41:46,270 --> 00:41:48,796
A man's life is at stake.

697
00:41:49,173 --> 00:41:53,611
If you believe beyond a reasonable
doubt this man killed his wife,

698
00:41:53,778 --> 00:41:55,747
then stick to your vote.

699
00:41:56,481 --> 00:41:59,713
But don't stick to your
vote for the wrong reason.

700
00:42:22,807 --> 00:42:25,140
Okay, you can call the bailiff.

701
00:42:53,671 --> 00:42:56,402
Members of the jury,
have you reached a verdict?

702
00:42:56,574 --> 00:42:57,814
We have, Your Honor.

703
00:42:57,942 --> 00:43:02,107
We, the jury, in the matter of
<i>The People v. Travis Spalding,</i>

704
00:43:02,280 --> 00:43:04,078
on the charge of
murder in the first degree,

705
00:43:04,248 --> 00:43:06,717
find the defendant not guilty.

706
00:43:10,421 --> 00:43:13,323
Thank you, members of
the jury. You are dismissed.

707
00:43:13,491 --> 00:43:14,550
This court is adjourned.

708
00:43:23,568 --> 00:43:28,336
Oh, Henrietta. Henrietta,
wait. Wait, wait, wait.

709
00:43:28,840 --> 00:43:31,537
I wanted to say goodbye.

710
00:43:31,709 --> 00:43:33,905
How very kind of you.

711
00:43:34,078 --> 00:43:36,445
You were a great foreperson.

712
00:43:36,614 --> 00:43:38,810
Thank you so much.

713
